-- statement:
--   A MeanPayoff.Game is a finite directed multigraph with n>0 vertices and m edges, each edge having a tail, a head and an integer weight, where every vertex has at least one outgoing edge and each vertex is owned by the maximizer or the minimizer (a Boolean flag). A Strategy maps each finite history of edges and each vertex v to an outgoing edge of v. Given strategies σ for the maximizer and τ for the minimizer and a start vertex, the play is generated by letting the owner of the current vertex choose the next edge from the history so far. meanPayoff of an infinite edge sequence is the liminf, as T→∞, of the average weight of its first T edges. A vertex v is Winning if some σ guarantees, against every τ, a mean payoff at least 0. Games are serialized by input: n and m, then the owner bits, then for each edge its tail+1, head+1 and weight, where encodeNat k writes the binary digits of k+1 (least significant first) preceded by their length in unary and a 0 separator, and encodeInt prefixes a sign bit to encodeNat of the absolute value. A Machine is a randomized three-tape Turing machine with finitely many states and a start state. Tapes are two-way infinite over blank, 0 or 1 symbols. Tape 0 initially holds the input from cell 0 onward. In each step the transition, based on the control state, the three symbols under the heads and one fresh random bit, either halts the machine or gives a next state, a symbol to write and a left, stay or right move for each tape. A halted configuration stays unchanged. result runs M on input G for T steps, reading random bit j at step j (bits are given as a function on Fin T, padded with false). CorrectOutput requires that the machine has halted and that tape 2 has, in cell v for each vertex v<n, the symbol 1 exactly when v is Winning and the symbol 0 exactly when it is not, and is blank in every other cell. MainClaim is a defined proposition, not an established theorem. It asserts that there exist a machine M and a constant C>0 such that for every game G there is a time bound T≤2^(C(log₂(|input(G)|+2))²) with two properties: for every string of T random bits M halts within T steps, and at least 7/8 of the 2^T random strings produce a CorrectOutput.
